{
  "dom_id": "step-457894",
  "name": "A Worked Example",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251309/assets/0ac79071675ca17dbb25708f8131a0f1706c29ffe69c750969112fd6a097d1f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $|\\mathbf{a}|=4$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=5.$ How can we find $|\\mathbf{a+b}|?$ \n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251309/assets/dd9b407a04394d9648db4671788791a8cf615b7755c1efead6f637fe26a40562.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. But we can use the law of cosines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =16+25+20 \\\\  & =61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|\\mathbf{a+b}|=\\sqrt{61}.$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
